before:
```swift
final class Meter {
    var value: Int {
        didSet { print("\(oldValue) -> \(value)") }
    }
    init(value: Int) { self.value = value }
}
let meter = Meter(value: 2)
meter.value = 5
```
This prints 2 -> 5. Directly setting this property in its own initializer does not trigger its observer.

after:
```swift
final class Meter {
    var value: Int {
        didSet { print("\(oldValue) -> \(value)") }
    }
    init(value: Int) { self.value = value }
}
let meter = Meter(value: 2)
meter.value = 5
```
The output is `2 -> 5`. Setting the property directly inside its own initializer creates the starting value without running this observer.
